DATA={'IV/27': {'intro': 'A lattice in the complex plane is a discrete rank-two subgroup, and quotienting by it produces a compact complex torus. This is the analytic model of every genus-one Riemann surface after choosing a base point.', 'sections': [('Lattices', 'A lattice has form $\\Lambda=\\mathbb Z\\omega_1+\\mathbb Z\\omega_2$ with $\\omega_1/\\omega_2\\notin\\mathbb R$.'), ('Fundamental parallelogram', 'Every complex number is congruent modulo the lattice to a point in a chosen fundamental parallelogram, with boundary identifications.'), ('Complex torus', 'The quotient $\\mathbb C/\\Lambda$ inherits a compact Riemann-surface structure from translations.'), ('Quotient projection', 'The map $\\pi:\\mathbb C\\to\\mathbb C/\\Lambda$ is a holomorphic covering map.'), ('Change of lattice basis', 'Replacing a lattice basis by an $SL_2(\\mathbb Z)$ basis does not change the lattice.'), ('Normalized modulus', 'After scaling, a lattice can be represented by $\\mathbb Z+\\mathbb Z\\tau$ with $\\Im\\tau>0$.'), ('Modular equivalence', 'Different $\\tau$ values related by fractional linear $SL_2(\\mathbb Z)$ transformations describe isomorphic normalized tori.'), ('Holomorphic differentials', 'The form $dz$ descends to a nowhere-vanishing holomorphic differential on the torus.'), ('Genus one', 'The torus has Euler characteristic zero and genus one.')], 'theorems': [('Complex structure on the quotient', 'For a lattice $\\Lambda$, the quotient $\\mathbb C/\\Lambda$ has a unique Riemann-surface structure making the projection locally biholomorphic.', 'Choose disks small enough that distinct lattice translates are disjoint. Projection restricts to a homeomorphism on each such disk, and transition maps between lifted charts are translations, hence holomorphic.'), ('Compactness of the torus', '$\\mathbb C/\\Lambda$ is compact.', 'The closure of a fundamental parallelogram is compact and its projection surjects onto the quotient. A continuous image of a compact set is compact.'), ('Basis-change invariance', "If $(\\omega_1',\\omega_2')=(a\\omega_1+b\\omega_2,c\\omega_1+d\\omega_2)$ with an integral matrix of determinant $\\pm1$, then the generated lattice is unchanged.", 'The new generators are integral combinations of the old, and invertibility over $\\mathbb Z$ expresses the old generators as integral combinations of the new.')]}, 'IV/28': {'intro': 'Elliptic functions are meromorphic functions periodic with respect to a rank-two lattice. Compactness of the associated torus forces strong balance laws for zeros, poles, and residues.', 'sections': [('Double periodicity', 'An elliptic function satisfies $f(z+\\omega)=f(z)$ for every lattice period $\\omega\\in\\Lambda$.'), ('Descent to the torus', 'Elliptic functions are exactly meromorphic functions on $\\mathbb C/\\Lambda$ pulled back to the plane.'), ('Poles in a period cell', 'A nonconstant elliptic function must have poles because a holomorphic function on the compact torus would be constant.'), ('Residue balance', 'The sum of residues in a fundamental parallelogram is zero.'), ('Zero-pole balance', 'The number of zeros equals the number of poles in a period parallelogram, counted with multiplicity.'), ('Abel-type position constraint', 'The sum of zeros minus the sum of poles is a lattice element, counted with multiplicity.'), ('Constructing elliptic functions', 'Periodic principal parts must satisfy residue compatibility before a global elliptic function can exist.'), ('Even and odd examples', 'The Weierstrass $\\wp$-function is even; its derivative is odd.'), ('Function-field preview', "For a fixed lattice, elliptic functions are generated algebraically by $\\wp$ and $\\wp'$.")], 'theorems': [('Residue sum theorem', 'The sum of residues of an elliptic function in a fundamental parallelogram containing no poles on its boundary is zero.', 'Integrate around the boundary. Opposite edges cancel because the function is periodic and the orientations are opposite. The residue theorem then forces the interior residue sum to vanish.'), ('Equal zero and pole count', 'A nonzero elliptic function has equally many zeros and poles in a fundamental parallelogram, counted with multiplicity.', "Apply the argument principle to $f'/f$. This quotient is elliptic, so opposite-edge contour integrals cancel, leaving zero; hence zeros minus poles equals zero."), ('No single simple pole', 'There is no elliptic function with exactly one simple pole modulo the lattice.', 'Such a function would have one nonzero residue in a fundamental parallelogram, contradicting the residue-sum theorem.')]}, 'IV/29': {'intro': 'The Weierstrass $\\wp$-function is the canonical elliptic function attached to a lattice. Its carefully regularized lattice sum gives a doubly periodic even function with one double pole per period cell and an algebraic differential equation.', 'sections': [('Regularized lattice sum', 'Define $\\wp(z)=z^{-2}+\\sum_{\\omega\\in\\Lambda\\setminus\\{0\\}}[(z-\\omega)^{-2}-\\omega^{-2}]$.'), ('Normal convergence', 'The subtraction of $\\omega^{-2}$ improves convergence so the series converges normally on compact sets avoiding the lattice.'), ('Evenness', 'Pairing lattice points $\\omega$ and $-\\omega$ shows $\\wp(-z)=\\wp(z)$.'), ('Double periodicity', "The derivative $\\wp'$ is manifestly lattice periodic, and comparison constants show $\\wp$ itself is elliptic."), ('Pole structure', 'Modulo the lattice, $\\wp$ has one double pole at the origin with zero residue.'), ('Derivative', "The derivative $\\wp'$ is odd and has a triple pole at the lattice points."), ('Half-period values', "At nonzero half-periods, $\\wp'$ vanishes; their $\\wp$ values are the roots $e_1,e_2,e_3$ of the cubic relation."), ('Differential equation', "There are lattice invariants $g_2,g_3$ with $(\\wp')^2=4\\wp^3-g_2\\wp-g_3$."), ('Second derivative relation', "Differentiating the cubic gives $\\wp''=6\\wp^2-g_2/2$."), ('Uniformization preview', "The map $z\\mapsto(\\wp(z),\\wp'(z))$ parametrizes a nonsingular cubic curve when the discriminant is nonzero.")], 'theorems': [('Normal convergence of the Weierstrass series', 'The defining series for $\\wp$ converges normally on compact subsets of $\\mathbb C\\setminus\\Lambda$.', 'For bounded $z$ and large $|\\omega|$, expand $(z-\\omega)^{-2}-\\omega^{-2}=O(|z|/|\\omega|^3)$. The lattice sum $\\sum_{\\omega\\ne0}|\\omega|^{-3}$ converges, so the Weierstrass M-test applies away from lattice points.'), ('Elliptic differential equation', "There exist constants $g_2=60\\sum_{\\omega\\ne0}\\omega^{-4}$ and $g_3=140\\sum_{\\omega\\ne0}\\omega^{-6}$ such that $(\\wp')^2=4\\wp^3-g_2\\wp-g_3$.", "Compare Laurent expansions at zero. The difference $(\\wp')^2-4\\wp^3+g_2\\wp+g_3$ is elliptic and has its principal part cancelled, hence is entire on the torus and therefore constant. The chosen coefficients make the constant zero."), ('Half-period critical points', "If $2a\\in\\Lambda$ but $a\\notin\\Lambda$, then $\\wp'(a)=0$.", "Because $\\wp'$ is odd and periodic, $\\wp'(a)=\\wp'(a-2a)=\\wp'(-a)=-\\wp'(a)$."), ('Degree-two map to the sphere', 'The function $\\wp$ induces a degree-two meromorphic map $\\mathbb C/\\Lambda\\to\\widehat{\\mathbb C}$, branched at the three nonzero half-periods and the origin.', 'Generically $\\wp(z)=\\wp(-z)$ gives two points in a fiber. Fixed points of the involution $z\\mapsto-z$ modulo the lattice are exactly the four half-period classes, where the local multiplicity rises to two.')]}, 'IV/30': {'intro': "Addition formulas express the group structure of the complex torus directly in the values of $\\wp$ and $\\wp'$. They are the analytic form of the chord-and-tangent law on the associated cubic curve.", 'sections': [('Translation on the torus', 'Addition of complex arguments modulo the lattice is the group law on $\\mathbb C/\\Lambda$.'), ('Need for an addition law', 'Since $\\wp$ is even, $\\wp(u+v)$ cannot be expressed from $\\wp(u),\\wp(v)$ alone without derivative data.'), ('Weierstrass addition formula', "A rational expression in $\\wp(u),\\wp(v),\\wp'(u),\\wp'(v)$ gives $\\wp(u+v)$."), ('Duplication formula', 'Taking $v\\to u$ yields the tangent or duplication formula.'), ('Subtraction formula', 'Replacing $v$ by $-v$ gives a companion formula for $\\wp(u-v)$.'), ('Pole comparison proof', 'Differences of candidate expressions can be shown constant by comparing elliptic principal parts.'), ('Cubic geometry', 'The slope term in the addition formula is the slope of the chord joining points on the Weierstrass cubic.'), ('Special values', "Half-periods and torsion points simplify the formulas because $\\wp'$ may vanish."), ('Division phenomena', 'Iterating addition generates rational functions describing multiplication-by-$n$ on the elliptic curve.')], 'theorems': [('Weierstrass addition formula', "For $u,v,u\\pm v\\notin\\Lambda$ and $\\wp(u)\\ne\\wp(v)$, $\\wp(u+v)=-\\wp(u)-\\wp(v)+\\frac14\\left(\\frac{\\wp'(u)-\\wp'(v)}{\\wp(u)-\\wp(v)}\\right)^2$.", 'As a function of one variable, compare both sides after shifting by the other argument. Their poles and principal parts agree; the difference is an elliptic function without poles, hence constant. Evaluate at a convenient limiting point to determine the constant.'), ('Duplication formula', "The limit $v\\to u$ in the addition formula gives $\\wp(2u)=-2\\wp(u)+\\frac14(\\wp''(u)/\\wp'(u))^2$ whenever the displayed quotient is defined.", "Apply l'Hôpital's rule to the chord-slope quotient as $v\\to u$."), ('Chord interpretation', "Under $z\\mapsto(\\wp(z),\\wp'(z))$, the analytic addition formula agrees with the chord-and-tangent law on $y^2=4x^3-g_2x-g_3$.", "The squared chord slope determines the third intersection's $x$-coordinate by Vieta's formula; reflection in the $x$-axis changes the sign of $y$, matching the torus inverse $z\\mapsto-z$.")]}, 'IV/31': {'intro': 'The Weierstrass parametrization identifies a complex torus with a smooth projective cubic curve. Analytic addition on the torus becomes the algebraic group law of an elliptic curve.', 'sections': [('Weierstrass cubic', 'The equation $y^2=4x^3-g_2x-g_3$ defines a nonsingular cubic when $g_2^3-27g_3^2\\ne0$.'), ('Parametrization', "The map $z\\mapsto[\\wp(z):\\wp'(z):1]$ extends across the lattice point to the point at infinity."), ('Point at infinity', 'The unique point at infinity is the identity of the cubic group law.'), ('Injectivity modulo sign and derivative', "The pair $(\\wp,\\wp')$ separates torus points modulo the lattice."), ('Surjectivity', 'Every point of the smooth cubic arises from a torus point.'), ('Biholomorphism', 'The extended Weierstrass map is a holomorphic bijection between compact Riemann surfaces and hence a biholomorphism.'), ('Group law', 'Addition of arguments corresponds to the chord-and-tangent group law on the cubic.'), ('Invariant differential', 'The form $dx/y$ pulls back to $dz$ up to the normalization determined by the chosen cubic equation.'), ('Moduli viewpoint', 'Changing the lattice by scaling changes $g_2,g_3$ with weights and preserves the modular invariant $j$.'), ('Genus-one synthesis', 'Complex tori, smooth cubic curves, elliptic functions, and genus-one Riemann surfaces with a base point describe the same analytic geometry.')], 'theorems': [('Weierstrass map lies on the cubic', "For every $z\\notin\\Lambda$, the pair $(\\wp(z),\\wp'(z))$ satisfies $y^2=4x^3-g_2x-g_3$.", 'This is exactly the differential equation for $\\wp$.'), ('Extension at the origin', "The map $z\\mapsto[\\wp(z):\\wp'(z):1]$ extends holomorphically at the lattice class of zero to the projective point at infinity.", "Using $\\wp(z)=z^{-2}+O(z^2)$ and $\\wp'(z)=-2z^{-3}+O(z)$, rescale homogeneous coordinates by a suitable power of $z$ to obtain a finite nonzero projective limit equal to the unique point at infinity."), ('Torus-cubic biholomorphism', 'If the discriminant is nonzero, the extended Weierstrass map $\\mathbb C/\\Lambda\\to E$ is a biholomorphism onto the smooth cubic $E$.', "The map is nonconstant and holomorphic between compact Riemann surfaces. The pair $\\wp,\\wp'$ separates points modulo the lattice, giving injectivity; compactness makes the image closed, while nonconstant holomorphic maps are open, so the image is all of the connected cubic. A holomorphic bijection between compact Riemann surfaces has holomorphic inverse."), ('Compatibility of group laws', 'Under the Weierstrass biholomorphism, addition on $\\mathbb C/\\Lambda$ corresponds to the cubic chord-and-tangent group law.', "The addition formula computes the coordinates of the image of $u+v$ and matches the algebraic chord construction; the identity is the lattice origin mapped to infinity and inversion is $z\\mapsto-z$, which changes the sign of $\\wp'$.")]}}
